DISCLOSED BEFORE THE RUN, not after: a simulated external buyer (the deployer wallet — not an agent, no model, no decisions) takes 3000 mSIU after window 1 and 3000 mSIU after window 2. These figures are fixed here in source before the run and written into the manifest; no agent's behaviour changes them.
ALSO DISCLOSED BEFORE THE RUN: window 3 is allowed to fail. If the pool is exhausted when it starts, the job does not get done and that is the result. Nothing in this script tops the pool back up, resizes a lot, or routes around the shortage.
AND: an undelivered claim CAN be settled in this run. settle_window_close is permissionless on-chain and is granted to the claim's holder and to both issuers — including against an issuer's own bond. Whether anyone actually notices a non-delivery and triggers it is a result of the run, not a property of it; capacity is returned and the bond pays only if someone does.
Window spans, fixed before the first turn (chain clock): w1 2026-10-05T16:49:11.000Z..2026-10-05T16:52:11.000Z; w2 2026-10-05T16:52:11.000Z..2026-10-05T16:55:11.000Z; w3 2026-10-05T16:55:11.000Z..2026-10-05T16:58:11.000Z. The runner waits for each to open; no inference is spent waiting.
DISCLOSED BEFORE THE RUN: ISSUER-A will not serve any redemption this run. It takes payment and holds real bonded capacity exactly as the other issuer does, but has no way to report a delivery — the same situation as an issuer whose delivery pipeline fails at the last step. Fixed in source before the run, not chosen to fit a result. Its claims will therefore default, and the question is whether the holder notices, triggers settle_window_close once the window closes, and is paid from that issuer's bond. No other agent is told this.
Default path REACHABLE in all 3 windows: every window closes on 2026-10-05, the day print 2026-10-05-commodity is dated. A claim left unserved can be settled and its issuer's bond drawn.
